{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "Crime and Punishment", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Computer Whiz Tried", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.3", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Identify and describe rules and obligations though a wider range of language and expressions in both work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands important and specific information in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Modal Verbs in the Passive Voice", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use modal verbs to add meaning to other verbs. They come before the dictionary form (Infinitive without 'to') of the main verb.  Modal verbs can add meaning connected with certainty or obligation.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "His actions must be reported to his probation officer during that period.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "He claimed that he wanted to show that computer networks could be easily infected. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "These laws, as written, had to be applied only to criminals who intentionally caused destruction by viruses. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You can use modal verbs in the passive voice. <br /> You use the passive voice when you want to focus on the receiver of the action, rather than the 'doer' of the action. <br /><br />  Here is the structure of a passive sentence:<br /><br />  Subject + To be + past participle<br /><br />  For example:<br />  <span class=\"italic\">Computer networks <span class=\"strong\">are</span> easily <span class=\"strong\">infected</span> by viruses.</span><br /><br />  You can add a modal verb before the <span class=\"italic\">To be</span>.<br /><br />  Subject + modal + To be + past participle<br /><br />  For example:<br />  <span class=\"italic\">Computer networks <span class=\"strong\">can be</span> easily <span class=\"strong\">infected</span> by viruses.</span> <br /><br />  Here the sentence now has an additional meaning of possibility, thanks to the modal <span class=\"strong\">can</span>.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "The Present Perfect Continuous Tense vs. Present Perfect Tense", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Present Perfect Continuous tense to talk about an action that began in the past and is still continuing to the present time.<br /><br />You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about a past action that has a connection to the present time. The action either finished in the past, just recently finished or, in some cases, continues to the present time.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "A talented young computer programmer has been tried and found guilty of computer virus crimes by a US court. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Jones has been made to pay $10,000.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The story has been garnering a lot of attention recently.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Jones, on the other hand, has claimed that he had not meant to cause damage.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The new laws have been causing \"computer geniuses\" to think twice before they create and spread viruses. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The main difference between these two tenses is that you use the Present Perfect Continuous when you want to emphasize that the action began in the past and <span class=\"strong\">is still continuing now</span>. <br /><br />  Some verbs are NOT used in the continuous form. Some common examples are: <span class=\"italic\">to be</span>, <span class=\"italic\">to have</span> and <span class=\"italic\">to know</span>.<br /><br />  You often use the Present Perfect Continuous with words that talk about a period of time continuing up to the present, like <span class=\"italic\">recently</span>, <span class=\"italic\">this week</span>, <span class=\"italic\">since March</span>, <span class=\"italic\">for the last two days</span>.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "whiz ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "genius", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A computer whiz was tried for computer crimes.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about crime and punishment.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to be tried", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to be brought to court for criminal charges", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A talented young computer programmer has been tried  for computer crimes.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "guilty", "type": "name"}, {"content": "responsible for a crime", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A computer programmer has been found guilty of computer crimes. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to crash", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to fail suddenly (when talking about computers)", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It caused over a billion dollars in damage by causing many computer systems to crash.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to garner", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to gather or collect ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The story has been garnering a lot of attention recently.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "destruction", "type": "name"}, {"content": "damage", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: These laws had to be applied only to criminals who intentionally caused destruction by viruses. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to think twice", "type": "name"}, {"content": " to consider a course of action carefully before doing it", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The new laws have been causing \"computer geniuses\" to think twice before they create and spread viruses. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud07", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to reproduce", "type": "name"}, {"content": "produce again", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: His \"Spider\" program reproduced much faster than Jones had expected.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud08", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "existing", "type": "name"}, {"content": "current", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The existing laws did not allow him to be punished. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03aud09", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn8le03ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Imagine that you are a computer hacker.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "What website would you hack into and why?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a list of websites you would like to hack.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your list with a partner. Do you have any of the same sites?", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I talk about rules and regulations.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about crime and punishment.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about things I have done or have been doing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my ideas with my partner.", "type": "text"}]}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about rules and regulations. ", 